{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicIsometryAffine","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicIsometryAffine","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicMetric","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.FDeriv.Add","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.FDeriv.Mul"],"declarations":9,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.dH_horizTranslation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.dH_horizTranslation","kind":"theorem","line":168,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicIsometryAffine.lean#L168-L175","doc":"**Horizontal translations preserve the hyperbolic distance.**  DERIVED via the\nopposite translation as the mutual inverse: `(x + b) + (-b) = x`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.horiz_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.horiz_neg","kind":"theorem","line":164,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicIsometryAffine.lean#L164-L166","doc":"The opposite of a horizontal vector is horizontal: `(-b)_z = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.horizTranslationIsom","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.horizTranslationIsom","kind":"def","line":147,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicIsometryAffine.lean#L147-L162","doc":"**The conformal height-scaling data of a horizontal translation** `p ↦ p + b`\nwith `b_z = 0`.  The derivative is the identity (`lam ≡ 1`), and the height is\nunchanged: `(p + b)_z = p_z + b_z = p_z`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.dH_dilation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.dH_dilation","kind":"theorem","line":135,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicIsometryAffine.lean#L135-L143","doc":"**Dilations preserve the hyperbolic distance.**  DERIVED via the reciprocal\ndilation as the mutual inverse: `s⁻¹·(s·x) = x`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.dilationIsom","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.dilationIsom","kind":"def","line":117,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicIsometryAffine.lean#L117-L133","doc":"**The conformal height-scaling data of a dilation** `p ↦ s·p` (`s > 0`).  The\nderivative is the scalar map `v ↦ s·v`, scaling every norm by `s`; and the height\nscales by the SAME `s`: `(s·p)_z = s·p_z`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.dH_heightFixingLinearIsom","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.dH_heightFixingLinearIsom","kind":"theorem","line":105,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicIsometryAffine.lean#L105-L113","doc":"**Horizontal rotations preserve the hyperbolic distance.**  A height-fixing\nlinear isometry `L` (a horizontal rotation, or the vertical reflection) is a\n`dH`-isometry, and `L.symm` is a height-fixing linear isometry inverse to it, so\n`dH (L x) (L y) = dH x y`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.heightFixing_symm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.heightFixing_symm","kind":"theorem","line":97,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicIsometryAffine.lean#L97-L103","doc":"The inverse of a height-fixing linear isometry is again height-fixing:\n`(L.symm p)_z = p_z` (apply `hL` at `L.symm p` and cancel `L (L.symm p) = p`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.heightFixingLinearIsom","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.heightFixingLinearIsom","kind":"def","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicIsometryAffine.lean#L75-L95","doc":"**The conformal height-scaling data of a height-fixing linear isometry**\n(`lam ≡ 1`).  Any linear isometry `L` whose action fixes the height coordinate\n(`(L p)_z = p_z`) scales every tangent norm by `1` (it is an isometry) and scales\nthe height by `1` (it fixes it), so it preserves the line element `‖v‖/z`.\nHorizontal rotations of the `(x,y)`-plane are exactly this. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.dH_eq_of_leftInverse","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.dH_eq_of_leftInverse","kind":"theorem","line":60,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicIsometryAffine.lean#L60-L71","doc":"**`dH` is preserved by a pair of mutually-inverse `dH`-isometries.**  Neither\ngenerator here is involutive, so `IsDHIsometry.dH_eq` (which needs an involution)\ndoes not apply; instead we sandwich `dH (φ x) (φ y)` between `dH x y` (from `φ`)\nand itself (from a left inverse `ψ`).  If `ψ` is also a `dH`-isometry and\n`ψ ∘ φ = id`, then `dH (φ x) (φ y) = dH x y`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}